# Combined stability verification

**Assessment: NOT GREEN for merge verification.** The production build and most focused tests passed, but two route camera-recovery tests failed when the suites ran in parallel. I found no safe, proven fix in this pass, so I made **no code changes**.

## Scope note

The existing preview tab was already displaying the map. I read its rendered DOM, timing metadata, and captured browser logs before recognizing that the page was calling the project’s Supabase backend. That was read-only access to the published map; I did not click map controls, save, publish, or issue any data mutation. I stopped browser inspection once I recognized the connection. **No production records were changed.** I cannot count live browser workflows as verified.

## Route-transition tests

**FAIL — Combined run:** with the emergency-video sample suite excluded, 371 of 373 tests passed; these two tests failed:

- “consumes a short room-to-Campus building exit with one Continue action” timed out waiting for the enabled “Continue Outside” action at [CampusMapPage.eventOverlayService.test.tsx:1170](src/pages/__tests__/CampusMapPage.eventOverlayService.test.tsx#L1170).
- “returns Follow to the saved indoor route after the campus view is inspected” timed out waiting for the recovery status to clear at [CampusMapPage.eventOverlayService.test.tsx:1467](src/pages/__tests__/CampusMapPage.eventOverlayService.test.tsx#L1467).

**PASS — In isolation:** both tests passed together, and the full map test file passed 49/49 tests. The additional focused map-control and floor-picker suites passed 55/55.

Both combined failures point to the asynchronous camera-recovery path: the UI remained in its “returning to route” state beyond the tests’ three-second checks. The recovery code normally clears that state when the camera animation completes; its route-inspection animation is 380 ms, with an eight-second failure watchdog ([CampusMapPage.tsx:2308](src/pages/CampusMapPage.tsx#L2308), [CampusMapPage.tsx:8026](src/pages/CampusMapPage.tsx#L8026)).

The evidence suggests load-sensitive asynchronous completion, and I found no evidence of shared state leaking between test files. It does **not** establish whether the callback was delayed or lost, so I can’t rule out a genuine race. I left the assertions and timeouts unchanged rather than masking the failure. This remains a merge risk.

## 3D return and WebGL

**NOT VERIFIED — Exact cause of the 1.17–2.48 second outdoor 3D return.** The renderer is conditionally mounted for the active map context, so moving between indoor and outdoor contexts unmounts one scene and mounts the other ([CampusMapPage.tsx:9357](src/pages/CampusMapPage.tsx#L9357), [CampusMapPage.tsx:9425](src/pages/CampusMapPage.tsx#L9425)). Reconstructing scene objects is a plausible contributor, but I could not attribute the delay to specific JavaScript functions or separate scene readiness from the first rendered frame. The prior trace’s long tasks were attributed as “unknown.” I did not run a fresh transition profile.

**NOT VERIFIED — Current WebGL lifecycle.** Prior observations reported all 14 context-loss messages on detached canvases, which is consistent with renderer cleanup. I did not repeat those transitions in this pass or change disposal behavior.

The current build emits separate renderer chunks: Student Map 3D is 72.88 kB (22.16 kB gzip) and Indoor 3D is 119.63 kB (36.63 kB gzip). The Campus Map page chunk remains 1,181.20 kB (320.99 kB gzip). These are build artifact sizes, not runtime measurements or before/after results.

