The trace identifies the cause: in Vitest’s jsdom environment, the animation starts with Node’s global `performance.now()`, while jsdom’s native RAF callback supplies a window-clock timestamp. In the failing worker those clocks differed by about 3.19 seconds, just beyond the tests’ 3-second condition; the animation kept receiving frames, and I observed no cancellation or lost callback. Browsers use the same clock for both APIs, so this is a test-harness synchronization defect rather than a reproduced application race. I’ll align RAF timestamps in this test file and add a small check that guards the harness assumption.
